  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places high pressure in the eastern\ntwo-thirds of the state. There also sits a low pressure in\ncentral Montana. Aloft a shortwave ridge axis is centered over\nMontana and moving eastward. Over Washington State a trough and\nlow pressure is digging into the CONUS. Tonight the\naforementioned ridge axis will slide into western North Dakota,\nswitching the flow to our active southwest pattern. Isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms are possible tonight across the west\nand north.\n\nFriday and Saturday under the active southwest flow pattern,\nsevere weather is once again possible. Friday the surface low\nwill move into southern South Dakota along a stationary front\nthat stretches into Canada. With ample surface heating into the\nupper 80s and dew points in the mid 60s, models predict plenty\nof MUCAPE in western and central North Dakota, about 1000 to\n2000 J/kg. Shear looks to be plenty around 40kts. The marginal\nlevel 1 risk is across most of western and central North Dakota.\nIf severe storms organize ping pong size hail could occur and\n60mph winds. Saturday the exact same area is under the gun with\nthe marginal stretching across the same area. Global models\nagree that there will be significantly less MUCAPE, around 1000\nJ/kg or less, and 40 to 50 kts of shear. So the risk on Saturday\nis much lower. \n\nThere will be chances for storms all weekend as that active\nsouthwest flow continues. Sunday the low pressure from\nWashington slides into eastern Montana, creating strong\ndiffluent flow across the state. This could cause severe \nstorms, but SPC does not have a day 4 highlight yet, and models\ndo not agree on the CAPE profile. Some have plenty of CAPE,\nothers don't. For the rest of the forecast period mainly the \nsouthwest flow will stick around, leading to near daily chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms. Temperatures will be near normal \nin the 80s.",
